UPHELD(high): I could not refute this; the defect is real. On origin/ios-node, NodeService.swift:104-108 has start() doing `guard loop == nil`, then applyIdleTimer(), and only then `loop = Task {...}`. applyIdleTimer() (line 117-119) sets `UIApplication.shared.isIdleTimerDisabled = keepAwake && loop != nil`. When it runs from start(), `loop` is still nil, so the flag is always set to false.

I searched the whole ios tree on origin/ios-node for `isIdleTimerDisabled` and `applyIdleTimer`. Nothing else writes the flag. The only other callers are stop() (line 114), which sets it false on purpose after `loop = nil`, and the keepAwake didSet (line 36), which runs only when the toggle changes. runLoop() never calls it again.

So every way into serving leaves the idle timer on: setServing(true), resumeIfEnabled() at launch, and scenePhaseChanged(.active). The only exception is the user flipping the keep-awake toggle while the loop is running. An unattended iPad will auto-lock, the scene goes to .background, and the handler at lines 81-92 calls stop(). Serving stops and any in-flight task is cancelled.

Severity: high fits, since unattended serving is the main use of this feature. | fix: In NodeService.start(), assign the loop before applying the idle timer:
```swift
private func start() {
    guard loop == nil else { return }
    loop = Task { [weak self] in await self?.runLoop() }
    applyIdleTimer()
}
```
Alternatively, applyIdleTimer could compute the flag from `isServing`, but the scene-pause path does not clear isServing, so keeping `loop != nil` and reordering is the safer change.

UPHELD(high): I could not refute this; the code on origin/ios-node behaves exactly as claimed.

- **The flag is set while `loop` is still nil.** `start()` (NodeService.swift:104-108) runs `guard loop == nil`, then calls `applyIdleTimer()`, and only then assigns `loop = Task {...}`. `applyIdleTimer()` (117-119) computes `keepAwake && loop != nil`. At that moment `loop` is guaranteed nil by the guard, so `isIdleTimerDisabled` is always set to false.
- **Nothing sets it again.** `git grep` on the branch finds only three calls to `applyIdleTimer()`:
  - the `keepAwake` didSet (36), which runs only when the user flips the toggle;
  - `start()` (106);
  - `stop()` (114), which runs after `loop = nil` and so also sets false.

  No other file touches `isIdleTimerDisabled`. `NodeSettingsSection.swift:11` only binds the toggle.
- **Every start path hits the bug.** Turning on Serve, `resumeIfEnabled()` at launch, and `scenePhaseChanged(.active)` all end in `start()`, so the screen is never kept awake. The one exception is a user who flips the keep-awake toggle off and on while already serving.
- **The failure follows.** iPadOS auto-lock sends the scene to `.background`. The `.background` branch (81-92) then calls `stop(status: "Paused (app in background)")`, which cancels the loop and any task in flight. Unattended serving therefore stops at the first auto-lock.

High severity is justified, because unattended serving is the node's main job. | fix: In start(), assign the task before applying the timer:
```swift
private func start() {
    guard loop == nil else { return }
    loop = Task { [weak self] in await self?.runLoop() }
    applyIdleTimer()
}
```
An alternative is to have applyIdleTimer() use `keepAwake && isServing && loop != nil` and call it after the assignment. That keeps stop() correct, because stop() clears loop before it calls applyIdleTimer().

UPHELD(high): I could not refute this. The core defect is real: GPUGate.acquire waits in a way that ignores task cancellation.

Evidence on origin/ios-node:
- ios/AgentConsole/Inference/GPUGate.swift:15-22: when the gate is busy, acquire does `await withCheckedContinuation { waiters.append((who, $0)) }`. The continuation type is `CheckedContinuation<Void, Never>`. There is no withTaskCancellationHandler and no way to remove a waiter. The only place a waiter is resumed is release() (removeFirst + resume). A cancelled caller therefore stays parked until the current holder releases the gate.
- A fleet task can hold the gate for a long time. NodeService.execute (NodeService.swift:189-208) holds it for the whole generate or train_lora run. runTrain sets `iterations = max(p.iterations ?? 50, 1)` with no upper bound (line 353), and ensureLoaded, adapter download and training all happen inside the gate. The loop's `holder` check (line 146) only stops the node from claiming work while chat already holds the gate. It does nothing for a chat that arrives after a fleet task has started.

Scenario 1 (chat) holds:
- send() sets isRunning=true and only clears it after run() returns. run() is stuck in the first `withGPU { ... }` acquire.
- stop() and reset() only call task?.cancel(), which cannot wake the waiter.
- ChatView shows Stop instead of Send while isRunning is true, and send() has `guard !isRunning`. So Stop does nothing, and after "New chat" no new message can be sent until the fleet task releases the gate.
- One detail is overstated. Once the gate is granted, the first withGPU body (load, then makeConversation) runs with no cancellation check, so it builds a conversation and assigns self.conversation, even after reset() cleared it. But `try Task.checkCancellation()` at the top of the tool loop then throws before conversation.send, so the user message is never prefilled. The turn ends with "Stopped."

Scenario 2 (background batch) holds:
- DocumentSummarizer.summarize (BatchSummarizer.swift:13) calls acquire with no cancellation path.
- ContinuedProcessing.swift:98-104: expirationHandler calls work.cancel(), then the code waits on `await work.value` before task.setTaskCompleted. If the batch is waiting behind a fleet task, which can happen while the app is in the foreground and the node is serving, completion is delayed until the gate is released.
- The same applies to pauseForeground() for a foreground batch.

Scenario 3 (node loop) is plausible but secondary. Nothing elsewhere guards against any of this, and severity medium is appropriate.

The proposed fix needs one correction. A cancellation handler can run before the waiter is appended, or after release() has already handed the gate to that waiter. Both cases must be handled, or a cancel can lose the gate or leave it held forever. | fix: Give each waiter an ID and make acquire throwing:

```swift
func acquire(_ who: String) async throws {
    try Task.checkCancellation()
    if !busy { busy = true; holder = who; return }
    let id = UUID()
    try await withTaskCancellationHandler {
        try await withCheckedThrowingContinuation { c in
            if Task.isCancelled { c.resume(throwing: CancellationError()); return }
            waiters.append((id, who, c))
        }
    } onCancel: {
        Task { await self.cancelWaiter(id) }
    }
}

private func cancelWaiter(_ id: UUID) {
    if let i = waiters.firstIndex(where: { $0.id == id }) {
        waiters.remove(at: i).resume.resume(throwing: CancellationError())
    }
}
```

If the waiter has already been handed the gate, cancelWaiter finds nothing to remove. In that case acquire returns normally, and the caller must release the gate as usual; its next checkCancellation then fails.

Call sites:
- withGPU, DocumentSummarizer.summarize and the federated-adapter path: write `try await GPUGate.shared.acquire(..)` outside the do/catch, so release() only runs when acquire succeeded.
- NodeService.execute: handle a thrown acquire by reporting a failure without calling release().
- In LocalAgent.run, add `try Task.checkCancellation()` right after acquire inside withGPU (before makeConversation), so a turn that was stopped does not build a stale conversation.
- Cap `iterations` (and batchSize) in runTrain.
